Subject: virtual-fs: synchronous, positional host files and stdio
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`host_fs::File` ran every read, write and seek as a `spawn_blocking`
task on the runtime handle captured at construction, and the stdio
wrappers did the same through `tokio::io`. In a forked child that
runtime has no threads: the first guest thread to write there blocks
forever. All of it is now done synchronously on the calling thread.

Files also keep their position in process memory and use `pread` and
`pwrite`: a forked child shares the kernel's file offset with its parent
and siblings through the inherited open file description, and a
seek-then-read in one process read whatever page another had moved the
offset to.

Synchronous I/O is the simplest way to keep a guest thread's I/O
independent of a runtime that fork cannot carry across, and it fits a
model where each guest thread is an OS thread the host owns. The
alternative — looking the runtime handle up at call time and
re-submitting work in flight on the child's runtime, as the host's
fork-aware sleep does for timers — would keep every operation a round
trip through a thread pool; it remains possible if that ever matters.
The positional reads and writes are not a choice: clones share the
kernel's file offset whichever thread does the reading.

